<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Server log verbosity and rotation.

  The 24.12 image ships <level>trace</level> with <size>1000M</size> and
  <count>10</count>. On this server that meant clickhouse-server.log growing
  ~2.8 GiB/day — engine telemetry (MergeTreeSequentialSource, MemoryTracker,
  per-SELECT pipeline traces) for a database holding ~245 MiB of actual data,
  and ~900 MiB of live + rotated logs sitting on a 30 GB disk.

  That write volume is billed. Azure meters managed-disk operations at the
  block layer and does not care whether a write carries table data or log
  text, so the log file alone accounted for roughly 20M operations/month
  (~$4/mo) once application writes had been gated on real changes.

  Note this is a SEPARATE channel from system.text_log (disabled in
  system_logs_ttl.xml). Turning off the table left the file untouched, which
  is why disk operations only fell by a third after that change.

  warning keeps startup/shutdown notices, config problems, failed queries and
  every error — i.e. what is actually consulted when something breaks — while
  dropping the per-operation trace chatter. Drop to information temporarily
  if a problem needs more context, or to debug/trace while reproducing a bug;
  both are safe to run briefly, just not as a steady state.

  size/count also bound the worst case: 3 x 100M instead of 10 x 1000M, so
  logs can occupy at most ~300 MiB rather than potentially filling the disk.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <logger>
        <level>warning</level>
        <size>100M</size>
        <count>3</count>
    </logger>
</clickhouse>
